export const lessons=[
{id:'first',track:'Investing essentials',title:'What are you actually buying?',minutes:12,icon:'layers',summary:'Stocks, bonds and funds — without the alphabet soup.',body:[['Ownership, lending, and a basket','A stock is a share of ownership in a company. A bond is a debt investment: the issuer promises payments under specified terms, but may default. A fund pools investors’ money into a portfolio. An ETF is a fund whose shares trade on an exchange.'],['The price is not the whole story','A $20 share is not automatically cheaper than a $200 share. Compare the price with earnings, assets, cash flows and the number of shares. A good company can still be a poor investment at an excessive price.'],['Try it','Write one sentence about what you own when you buy a share of a company, and another about what you own when you buy a broad-market fund.']],question:'Which statement describes a stock?',options:['A guaranteed-interest bank deposit','An ownership interest in a company','A promise that your money cannot fall in value'],answer:1,explain:'A stock represents ownership. Its price and dividends are not guaranteed.',sources:['stocks','investing']},
{id:'risk',track:'Investing essentials',title:'Risk, time and diversification',minutes:12,icon:'shield',summary:'Build a plan that can survive more than one outcome.',body:[['Start with the goal','Money needed for next semester’s tuition has a different time horizon from retirement savings. A volatile investment may force you to sell at a loss when the bill arrives. Separate near-term needs from long-term goals.'],['Spread the sources of risk','Diversification spreads exposure across investments. Ten companies in one industry may share the same risks. A diversified fund can simplify ownership, but its holdings, strategy and fees still deserve review. Diversification cannot eliminate market losses.'],['Make risk concrete','Ask: if this account fell 30%, would I need the money, panic-sell, or be able to stick with my plan? Your willingness to take risk and your ability to absorb losses are different.']],question:'Which is the strongest reason to avoid investing tuition due next month in stocks?',options:['Stocks are always bad investments','The money has a short time horizon and can fall before it is needed','Diversification guarantees a profit'],answer:1,explain:'The time when you need the money matters. Even a diversified stock portfolio can decline.',sources:['allocation']},
{id:'compound',track:'Investing essentials',title:'Compounding: give time a job',minutes:12,icon:'chart',summary:'Explore how contributions and returns interact over time.',body:[['Growth can earn growth','Compounding happens when returns remain invested and later returns apply to a larger balance. Regular contributions can also build wealth. Both time and costs affect the result.'],['A thought experiment','Imagine $1,000 invested for two years with a hypothetical 5% annual return. After one year it becomes $1,050; after two it becomes $1,102.50. This illustration assumes a constant return and ignores fees, taxes and inflation. Real markets do not move smoothly.'],['Use the lab','Change the monthly contribution, years and assumed return in the growth calculator. Compare the amount contributed with the hypothetical ending balance. A projection is a scenario, not a promise.']],question:'What does the compound-growth calculator show?',options:['A guaranteed future balance','A hypothetical result based on its assumptions','The best stock to buy'],answer:1,explain:'A calculation can be exact while its investment assumptions are uncertain.',sources:['investing']},
{id:'fees',track:'Investing essentials',title:'The small numbers that add up',minutes:12,icon:'coins',summary:'Understand fund expenses, trading costs and advisory fees.',body:[['Look beyond a commission','A zero trading commission does not mean an investment is free. Fund expenses, advisory charges, spreads and account fees can affect what you keep. Ask what is charged, when, and on what balance.'],['Compare like with like','A fund’s expense ratio is an annual operating cost as a percentage of assets. Two funds with similar names may have different strategies and costs. Read the prospectus and fee disclosures rather than choosing on a single metric.'],['Try it','At a constant $10,000 balance, a hypothetical 1% annual fee is $100 and a 0.1% fee is $10. Real balances change; recurring costs also reduce the amount available to compound.']],question:'A fund with a 1% annual expense ratio is best understood as…',options:['Guaranteed to return 1%','Having an annual operating cost measured as a percentage of assets','Charging exactly $1 regardless of account size'],answer:1,explain:'The expense ratio describes costs, not expected returns.',sources:['fees']},
{id:'statements',track:'Stock research',title:'Read a business, not just a ticker',minutes:12,icon:'document',summary:'Connect the income statement, balance sheet and cash flows.',body:[['Three views of one business','The income statement tracks revenue and expenses over a period. The balance sheet is a snapshot of assets, liabilities and equity. The cash-flow statement explains changes in cash through operating, investing and financing activities.'],['Profit is not cash','A business can record revenue before receiving payment, and capital spending uses cash differently from an income-statement expense. Compare trends and read the notes before declaring a company financially strong.'],['Your first research assignment','Open a company’s latest annual filing. Identify its business segments, a source of revenue, a significant cost, and one disclosed risk. Record the reporting period beside every number in your notebook.']],question:'Which statement is a snapshot at a particular date?',options:['Income statement','Cash-flow statement','Balance sheet'],answer:2,explain:'A balance sheet shows what the company owns and owes at a specific date.',sources:['filings']},
{id:'thesis',track:'Stock research',title:'Build a thesis you can challenge',minutes:12,icon:'search',summary:'Write down the upside, the risks and what would prove you wrong.',body:[['Separate a business from a trade','“I use this product” is a starting point for research, not a complete investment case. Ask how the company makes money, why customers choose it, and whether the current valuation already reflects optimistic expectations.'],['Use a repeatable structure','Write four short sections: business model, reasons for potential growth, valuation assumptions, and risks. Compare competitors and distinguish facts from estimates. Include a source and a date for any financial metric.'],['Define the counterargument','Before you become attached to a stock, write the strongest reason your thesis could fail. Decide what new evidence would change your view. Add the company to a research watchlist, then revisit the thesis after new filings.']],question:'Which makes an investment thesis more useful?',options:['Ignoring evidence that disagrees with it','Including risks and evidence that would change your view','Treating past returns as a guarantee'],answer:1,explain:'A useful thesis is testable and can be revised as evidence changes.',sources:['stocks','filings']},
{id:'budget',track:'Personal finance',title:'Give every dollar a purpose',minutes:12,icon:'wallet',summary:'Make a student budget flexible enough for real life.',body:[['Map what comes in and goes out','Start with reliable take-home income and support. List essentials, debt payments, flexible spending and savings. Convert irregular expenses such as books or travel into monthly amounts so they are not surprises.'],['Build a buffer','Emergency savings can help you handle an unexpected bill without relying on costly debt. The appropriate amount depends on your situation. Start with a feasible target and keep the money accessible.'],['Use a realistic practice budget','Try the budget lab with fictional numbers first. If spending exceeds income, identify a change in income, costs or timing. A budget is a planning tool to revisit, not a moral judgment about spending.']],question:'Which expense belongs in a realistic monthly budget?',options:['Only bills that arrive every month','Only entertainment','An allowance for irregular costs such as textbooks'],answer:2,explain:'Planning for irregular expenses helps avoid surprises.',sources:['emergency']},
{id:'account',track:'Personal finance',title:'Open your first investment account',minutes:12,icon:'building',summary:'From choosing an account to understanding what happens after funding.',body:[['Choose the account before the investment','A taxable brokerage account and an IRA are account types, not investments. Their tax rules, eligibility and withdrawal treatment differ. A cash account requires you to pay for purchases; a margin account involves borrowing and adds risk.'],['Do your checks','Compare providers’ services, fees and investment access. Verify the firm and professional through official regulatory tools. The provider may request identity, employment and financial information. Submit sensitive information only through the provider’s verified application.'],['After approval','Link a funding source, understand settlement and cash treatment, and read trade confirmations. Funding an account does not automatically invest the cash. Learn your order types and choose investments consistent with your own plan.']],question:'You transfer money into an empty brokerage account. What should you check next?',options:['Whether the cash has actually been invested or is still held as cash','Whether returns are now guaranteed','Whether you must enable margin'],answer:0,explain:'An account is a container. Depositing cash and purchasing an investment are different actions.',sources:['accounts','broker']},
{id:'ira',track:'Personal finance',title:'Roth IRA, traditional IRA, or brokerage?',minutes:12,icon:'compass',summary:'Learn the account differences before choosing a provider.',body:[['The container changes the rules','A taxable brokerage account generally has no IRA-style annual contribution cap, but taxes may apply to dividends, interest and realized gains. IRAs are retirement accounts with eligibility requirements, contribution limits and withdrawal rules.'],['Traditional and Roth','Traditional IRA contributions may be deductible depending on circumstances, and distributions are generally taxable. Roth contributions are not deductible; qualified distributions are tax-free. Tax treatment is conditional, not a blanket guarantee.'],['Check the current rules','IRA contributions generally require eligible compensation, subject to exceptions such as qualifying spousal contributions. Roth eligibility also depends on income. Review the IRS rules for the relevant tax year rather than assuming your account balance or student status determines eligibility.']],question:'Which statement about an IRA is accurate?',options:['Any student can contribute any amount without eligible compensation','An IRA is an account type with eligibility and contribution rules','An IRA guarantees a fixed investment return'],answer:1,explain:'Eligibility, annual limits and tax treatment apply to the account; returns depend on the investments held.',sources:['ira']},
{id:'macro',track:'Markets',title:'Read the market beyond the headlines',minutes:12,icon:'globe',summary:'Put interest rates, inflation and earnings in context.',body:[['Markets react to expectations','A headline can sound positive while prices fall if investors expected something even better. Distinguish the reported result, prior expectations and the market reaction before drawing conclusions.'],['Build a small dashboard','Track company earnings, interest rates, inflation and employment. Note the release time and reporting period. The Federal Reserve’s calendar and BLS releases help you find primary information instead of relying on a social-media summary.'],['Ask one better question','Rather than “will stocks go up?”, ask how a change could affect revenue, costs, borrowing or valuation for a particular business. Write down competing explanations. Correlation with a news event does not prove causation.']],question:'Why can a stock fall after reporting higher earnings?',options:['Higher earnings always mean a higher stock price','The result may be below what investors expected','Market prices never consider expectations'],answer:1,explain:'Prices incorporate expectations as well as reported results; multiple influences can operate at once.',sources:['fed','filings']}
];
